  "question": {
    "text": "A particle moves in a circle with a uniform speed. When it goes from a point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>&nbsp;to a diametrically opposite point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>, the momentum of the particle changes by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mover><mtext>P</mtext><mo>→</mo></mover><mtext>A</mtext></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mover><mtext>P</mtext><mo>→</mo></mover><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mover><mtext>j</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mtext>&nbsp;kg m&nbsp;s</mtext><mn>-1</mn></msup></math> &nbsp;and the centripetal force acting on it changes by <math> <mrow> <msub> <mrow> <mover accent=\"true\"> <mtext>F</mtext> <mo>→</mo> </mover> </mrow> <mtext>A</mtext> </msub> <mo>-</mo> <msub> <mrow> <mover accent=\"true\"> <mtext>F</mtext> <mo>→</mo> </mover> </mrow> <mtext>B</mtext> </msub> <mo>=</mo> <mn>8</mn> <mover accent=\"true\"> <mtext>i</mtext> <mo>^</mo> </mover> <mtext>&nbsp;N</mtext> </mrow> </math>. Where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">^</mo></mover></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></math> are unit vectors along <math><mi>x</mi></math> and <math><mi>y</mi></math> axes respectively. The angular velocity of the particle is",
